\begin{align*}(x+5) + (x+2) &= 2x+7>0\\ (x+5)(x+2) &> \left(\frac{(x+5)+(x+2)}{2}\right)^2 = \left(\frac{2x+7}{2}\right)^2 = \frac{4x^2 + 28x + 49}{4} > 0.\end{align*}
Thus $(x+5)(x+2) > \frac{4x^2 + 28x + 49}{4}$. Now divide by $x+1$ to get $\frac{(x+5)(x+2)}{x+1} > \frac{4x^2 + 28x + 49}{4(x+1)} = \frac{4x^2 + 4x + 28x + 49}{4x + 4} = \frac{4x(x+1) + 28(x+1) + 25}{4(x+1)} =(x+1)\dfrac 44 + 7 + \dfrac{25}{4(x+1)} = x + 1 + 7 + \dfrac{25}{4(x+1)} > 0$ by AM-GM. Thus $x= -1$ which is not possible. Therefore the minimum value of $\frac{(x+5)(x+2)}{x+1}$ under the assumption that $x>0$ is $\boxed{14}$</endthink> <answer> $14$</endanswer>.

